// Start at main, then read the topic modules. `mb run` prints
// Result: Ok(i32(42)) and `mb test` runs the checks described below.
// For a source declaration that calls a typed host import, see
// ../topics/host-capability/README.md. It requires the CLI profile in project.toml;
// the current source ABI supports only write_i32(i32) -> unit.
//
// For an import-free scalar component, see ../topics/component/README.md:
// `mb build --component` produces an application component for `mb exec`;
// `--lib --component` exposes selected scalar library functions. This guide's
// `i32 ! Error` entry also supports components; `bool ?! Error` is supported
// for application entries too. Derived errors and Unit/char outcome payloads
// remain gated. The console host
// import is supported via WIT; see ../topics/host-capability/README.md.
//
// The guide is split into topic modules next to this file. Each topic module
// documents its feature with comments and checks it with `test fn` items that
// use `assert`. Tests are type-checked by `mb check` and run by `mb test`;
// `mb build` and `mb run` leave them out of the artifact. This `main` is a
// small program that uses a few guide features and returns Ok(42).
//   numbers.mb         integers, floats, casts, wrapping and bit operations
//   text.mb            char, string literals, bytes, scalars, loops, concat
//   tuples.mb          tuple values, destructuring, tuples with owned strings
//   aggregates.mb      records, anonymous shapes, opaque types, share fields
//   ownership.mb       string-owning structs, borrow/share calls, drop
//   collections.mb     fixed arrays, byte arrays, lists, iteration
//   enums_outcomes.mb  enums, match, optionals, references, `T ! E` and `T ?! E`
//   generics.mb        generic functions, structs, methods, interface bounds
//   functions.mb       function types, anonymous functions, `=>` bodies, calls
//   operators.mb       user-defined core::ops operators and compound assignment

use collections::{Row, total, make_words};
use text::byte_length;

// Project features to try from the repository root:
// - examples/topics/module-imports is the runnable multi-module setup for
//   `use math;` and explicit `point.draw() of Draw` selection. From the repo
//   root, run `mb run --project examples/topics/module-imports/project.toml`
//   or build that project and run its Wasm artifact. The grouped
//   `use self::main::{Point, Draw};` import loads symbols; only the direct
//   module edge activates public extensions. Transitive imports and prelude
//   aliases do not activate extensions, and external package loading is not
//   implemented yet. Its `exercise<T>` wrapper calls both an inherent method
//   and a selected interface method. Constrained generic evidence is selected
//   from the caller's visible extensions and forwarded through nested calls.
// - examples/topics/numeric imports a public function via self::math and
//   exports it as add in project.toml. Use its manifest with mb resolve --lib,
//   mb build --lib, or mb build --output PATH.
// - examples/topics/structs imports a public type and uses public fields.
//   Imports support qualified paths and as aliases. Items and fields are
//   module-private by default. A manifest prelude can supply local names.
// - examples/topics/arrays and examples/topics/enums extend the aggregate
//   examples. examples/analysis has standalone analyze and ownership inputs.
// - crates/memobits-cli/README.md covers project, syntax, resolve, analyze,
//   ownership, analyze-project, ownership-project, build, and run. See it
//   for stack_size, fuel limits, and selecting a Wasm export with --entry.
//   The runner calls zero-argument exports. Library exports use scalar
//   signatures. Source host linking is limited to the CLI profile's typed
//   memobits:console.write_i32(i32) -> unit import; see the host-capability
//   example. Type exports and mb check are unsupported.
// - docs/design/proposed-language-guide.mb (a historical proposal) explores target syntax. String slicing,
//   scalar iteration, lists, try, defaults and
//   aggregate .copy() are gated. Generic function calls and interface evidence
//   forwarding are limited to the examples above; generic nominal structs are
//   demonstrated above.

// A zero-argument application entry can return `T ! E` or `T ?! E` with a
// primitive success payload. Both runners show the variant and typed value
// or the concrete error type and code.
//
// This main combines three guide features: a fixed array summed through a
// borrow (collections), the UTF-8 byte length of a string (text), and the
// first element of a List built by a helper (collections).
fn main() -> i32 ! Error {
    let row: Row = [10, 12, 8];
    let readings := total(row.borrow()); // 30
    let label := "aé€";
    let label_bytes := byte_length(label.borrow()); // 1 + 2 + 3 = 6
    let words := make_words(6);
    // `try x else { ... }` unwraps a `T?` or `T ! E`; the handler yields a
    // fallback value or exits, as `return` does here.
    let first := try words.get(0) else { return Err(Error { code: 1 }); };
    Ok(readings + label_bytes as i32 + first as i32)
}
